# A type of [[Central European|Central]] and [[Eastern European]] [[pilsner]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1946|journal=The North American Union News: Official Organ North American Union Life Assurance Society|volume=50–54|page=!19 and unknown page|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=S52A2i-uKp8C&q=pivo|passage=Remember boys—I’m telling you these games are getting livelier and more exciting as time goes on and also remember the more members that stay at home the more '''pivo''' and sandwiches we attending members have—not only that but all the fun we are having at the same time, is that something to miss—NO NO NO NO —but if you boys want to stay at home, remember the woe is all yours. {{...}} We can honestly say this meeting was worth while attending, pulenty{{sic}} of '''pivo''' and eats of all kinds.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=21 December 1991|author=Paul M. Cole|chapter=Palm Trees in Moscow|title=POW/MIA Accounting|volume=!I (Searching for America’s Missing Servicemen in the Soviet Union)|location=Singapore|publisher=w:Palgrave Macmillan|year_published=2018|section=chapter 6 (To Moscow via Stockholm and Helsinki)|page=268|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mQxWDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA268|isbn=978-981-10-7127-0|passage=The guys had a case of American Beer from the Pittsburgh brewing company and some Russian '''pivo''' (beer – tasted like Pilsner Urquell).}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2001|author=w:Bob Hicok|title=Three Poems|journal=w:Poetry Northwest|volume=42–43|location=Seattle, Wash.|publisher=Patrician Press|page=22|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FoE7AQAAIAAJ&q=pivo|issn=0032-2113|oclc=1762518|passage=But I’m out of coffee, down to three pieces of bread and one pair of shoes that were pillows on the train from Brussels to Belgrade when I didn’t speak the language of the veteran without an arm, we smoked slavic cigarettes and drank '''pivo''' and he showed me how to take clothes apart and turn them into a bed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=John F. Sieckhaus|chapter=The Journey Begins|title=Inching Toward Heaven’s Door: A Would-Be Alchemist’s Journey of Faith, Remembrance and Healing|location=[Bloomington, Ind.]|publisher=w:Xlibris|section=book I (An Adventure in Healing)|pages=31–32|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6Xkf5Ne3ByEC&pg=PA32|isbn=0-7388-9930-5|passage=We had a very emotional experience of Czech culture, viewing some of the best that the country had to offer in terms of its art and architecture at Prague, Karlstein and Krivoclat Castles, listening to the music of Smetana as performed by the Czechoslovak String Quartet in Liblice’s grand salon, and drinking '''pivo''' (pilsner beer) and slivovitz (plum brandy) in local restaurants where at one point we shared an unforgettable moment with a group of students in a highly risky (to them) rendition of “When the Saints Go Marching In”.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Eric Obenauf|title=Can You Hear Me Screaming?|location=[Columbus, Oh.]|publisher=[[w:Two Dollar Radio|Two Dollar Radio Publishing]]|pages=[https://books.google.com/books?id=cjfcv3lWaVMC&pg=PA124 124], [https://books.google.com/books?id=cjfcv3lWaVMC&pg=PA129 129], [https://books.google.com/books?id=cjfcv3lWaVMC&pg=PA134 134], and [https://books.google.com/books?id=cjfcv3lWaVMC&pg=PA136 136]|isbn=0-9763895-0-9|passage=For a while I sat in a chair behind him drinking some local '''pivo''', pretending not to read what he had written. {{...}} I looked over at Max, the romantic retard, who just rolled his eyes, finished off his '''pivo''', and left to get us another. {{...}} Everyone from the town was down at the restaurant/everything else, sitting outside at tables and smoking cigarettes and drinking coffee or wine or '''pivo''' or slivovice, while the children ran around the docks jumping from boat to boat to boat playing tag. {{...}} I tasted the wine coming out, the margherita pizza with the egg in the middle, the '''pivo''' from the ferry ride over.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Angus Bell|chapter=The Village That Beat Poland|title=Slogging the Slavs: A Paranormal Cricket Tour from the Baltic to the Bosphorus|location=Newcastle upon Tyne|publisher=Fat Controller Media|page=102|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sloggingslavspar0000bell/page/102/mode/1up|isbn=0-9554332-0-7|passage=I was overlooking the former Hotel Relax, my car papers safely at my side, a tall glass of '''pivo''' in hand.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Bernd Herzogenrath|chapter=Introduction: The Return of {{w|Edgar G. Ulmer}}|editor=Bernd Herzogenrath|title=Edgar G. Ulmer: Essays on the King of the B’s|location=Jefferson, N.C.|publisher=[[w:McFarland & Company|McFarland & Company, Inc.]]|page=6|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=-6kLAQAAMAAJ&q=pivo|isbn=978-0-7864-3700-9|passage=You only have to walk Olomouc streets (or better—the byroads and back-alleys of the old city center), and you are ''within'' a German Expressionist set! The more '''pivo''' or slivovice you’ve had, the more forced your perspectives grow. Go to the Tourist Office and see the Hanacka Giant, Olomouc’s closest to the Golem!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=17 December 2013|author=Dolly Niemiec Konwinski|coauthors=Douglas Williams|chapter=Acknowledgments|title=Summertime Dreams: Yes! Girls Can Play Baseball! Memories and Stories of a Professional Baseball Player|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=w:AuthorHouse|page=13|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_MyVAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA13|isbn=978-1-4918-4211-9|passage=While he would often tell folks his favorite was chocolate ice cream, with his second place prize going to a cold glass of '''pivo''', everyone knew Mom and his two daughters were always his true priorities.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=John Wilton|chapter=Janácek and a different type of tune – the music of youth|title=The Hope|location=London|publisher=PublishNation|page=165|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FrWWBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA165|isbn=978-1-291-80461-4|passage=Even sadder than the fact that by the time he left the bar where the captivating dirty blonde haired dancer had exhibited her talents so well the only people left in the place at around one a.m. were him and a well-built dodgy looking guy sat in one of the corners with his eyes fixed solidly on his half empty glass of '''pivo'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2024|author=Donald (Donny) N. Roberson, Jr.|title=The School of Travel: Twenty Years in Central Europe (Croatia and Czech Republic)|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Morgan James Publishing|section=sections “[https://books.google.com/books?id=lG36EAAAQBAJ&pg=PT130 Michal]” and “[https://books.google.com/books?id=lG36EAAAQBAJ&pg=PT194 Football/hooligans]”|isbn=9781636983523|passage=I taught him how to play backgammon, and we had many fun evenings drinking '''pivo''' and playing a competition of backgammon, as well as listening to some music and solving all the problems of the world. {{...}} Walking down one street, you would see hundreds of the fans with their same color shirts sitting in cafés and pubs drinking glass after glass of '''pivo''', and then on the other side of the street is the other team’s fans, doing the same.}}

